  "notes": "Baroque-pop-meets-emo — the most orchestrated corner of the pop-punk wave: 88–150 straight with the backbeat on 2&4 and the signature single at 88 in A minor, which is where `bpm.mode` sits. Harmony is i–VI–III–VII \"with a Neapolitan/chromatic passing chord\", which is authored literally as a `bII` degree in its own family. Three drum deltas are quoted: \"kick 1 and 3\", \"snare 2&4 with a rimshot alternative\" (the rim lane), and \"cymbal chokes and a marching-snare figure as the transition device\" — the crash/tom perc block with a 16th snare-roll ladder — plus the fourth fact, \"sections with no drums at all\", which is `dropOutBeats` at 0.92 over 8 beats. The bass is the production's fingerprint: \"played bass on roots **plus a pizzicato cello doubling the bass line** — the string bass is part of the rhythm section, not a pad\", so the bassline runs `independent_riff` at 0.85 with a staccato-friendly low anticipation, and the pizzicato strings are the counter running arp and answer-lick. The melody is documented as C4–A5 \"with a theatrical, wide-interval contour\", which is the 0.2 leap share — the widest in this section. ⚠ `confidence` is medium because the research rates it high-medium. ⚠ The **INFERRED** parameters are every drum probability; the 3/4 waltz sections the entry names are not encodable in a 4/4 generator and are recorded here instead.",
